    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>50cc Motorbike Hanoi: Solo Travelers Parking</h1>
    <p>Parking is the quiet half of every motorbike day in the capital. For solo travelers on a 50cc motorbike in Hanoi, parking decides where you stop, how long you stay, and how relaxed you feel when you walk away from the bike. The verified rate for a 50cc rental is 200,000 VND per day, the counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien is open from 09:00 to 21:00 daily, and a compact 50cc scooter is the easiest machine in the city to park, light enough to nudge into tight rows and small enough to fit where bigger bikes are waved away.</p>
    <p>This guide belongs to the <a href="/hanoi-50cc-motorbike-rental/">50cc motorbike rental hub for Hanoi</a>, and it sits beside the guide to <a href="/hanoi-50cc-motorbike-rental/solo-travelers-choosing-a-50cc/">choosing a 50cc as a solo traveler</a>. For what a rental should cost across days or weeks, see the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost overview</a>, and for contract basics the <a href="/faq/">rental FAQ</a> collects the common answers. Parking alone means you make every call yourself, where to leave the bike, what to take with you, and when a spot is worth a longer walk, and the sections below turn those calls into habits.</p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents"><ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">Why parking shapes a solo day</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">Reading a parking attendant's patch of street</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">Paid lots, yards, and building basements</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-4">Locks, keys, and what to leave on the bike</a></li>
